---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import ToolPage from "@bt/ui/ToolPage.astro";
import AdSlot from "@bt/ui/AdSlot.astro";
import Summary from "@bt/ui/sections/Summary.astro";
import SplitBar from "@bt/ui/sections/SplitBar.astro";
import Steps from "@bt/ui/sections/Steps.astro";
import Checklist from "@bt/ui/sections/Checklist.astro";
import FiniquitoCalc from "../../components/FiniquitoCalc.tsx";
---
<Layout
  bleed
  lang="en"
  title="Mexico finiquito and severance calculator 2026 | Baitaan"
  description="Work out your 2026 finiquito or severance under Mexico's Federal Labor Law: aguinaldo, vacation, 3 months' pay and seniority premium. Free, no sign-up."
>
  <ToolPage
    heading="Finiquito and severance calculator 2026 (Mexico)"
    lead="Whenever a job ends in Mexico, you are owed a finiquito: unpaid salary plus proportional aguinaldo, vacation and vacation premium. If you were dismissed without just cause, you are also owed severance (liquidación) of at least 3 months' pay."
    updated="Updated: November 2026 · 2026 minimum wage and UMA"
    sourcesHeading="Sources"
    sources={[
      { href: "https://www.diputados.gob.mx/LeyesBiblio/pdf/LFT.pdf", label: "Federal Labor Law, arts. 48–50, 76–80, 87, 162, 486" },
      { href: "https://www.gob.mx/profedet", label: "PROFEDET: free labor advice" },
    ]}
  >
    <FiniquitoCalc client:load lang="en" />

    <Fragment slot="guide">
      <Summary heading="In 30 seconds" items={[
        { label: "If you resign", title: "Finiquito only", text: "Unpaid salary, aguinaldo, vacation and vacation premium." },
        { label: "If you are dismissed without cause", title: "Finiquito and severance", text: "Plus 3 months’ pay and the seniority premium.", featured: true },
        { label: "To make a claim", title: "2 months", text: "From the day of the dismissal (LFT art. 518)." },
      ]} />

      <h2>Finiquito vs. severance</h2>
      <p>The <strong>finiquito</strong> is what your employer owes you for work already done. You get it whether you resign or are dismissed. <strong>Severance</strong> (liquidación) is extra compensation, owed only when you are dismissed without just cause or when you end the job because of the employer's fault (arts. 51 and 52).</p>

      <h2>What the finiquito includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unpaid salary:</strong> days worked and not yet paid.</li>
        <li><strong>Proportional aguinaldo:</strong> 15 days' pay per year (art. 87), in proportion to the days worked since 1 January.</li>
        <li><strong>Vacation:</strong> the proportional part of the current service year, plus untaken days from earlier years (arts. 76 and 79).</li>
        <li><strong>Vacation premium:</strong> at least 25% of that vacation pay (art. 80).</li>
        <li>Any other pending pay: profit sharing (PTU), bonuses or commissions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What severance includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>3 months' pay</strong> (90 days of integrated daily salary), arts. 48 and 50 sec. III.</li>
        <li><strong>20 days per year worked</strong> (art. 50 sec. II), when the employer refuses to reinstate you (art. 49) or you end the job because of the employer (art. 52).</li>
        <li><strong>Seniority premium:</strong> 12 days per year worked (art. 162). The salary used is capped at twice the minimum wage: $630.08 a day in 2026 ($881.74 in the northern border zone).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you win a lawsuit, you may also be owed back pay of up to 12 months (art. 48). The calculator leaves it out because it depends on the ruling.</p>

      <h2>What you get in each case</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted"><th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Item</th><th class="px-1.5 py-2 text-center font-medium">Resignation</th><th class="px-1.5 py-2 text-center font-medium">Justified dismissal</th><th class="px-1.5 py-2 text-center font-medium">Dismissal without cause</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">Unpaid salary</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">Proportional aguinaldo</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">Vacation and vacation premium</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">3 months’ pay</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">No</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">No</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">20 days per year</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">No</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">No</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">In some cases</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-hair"><td class="py-1.5">Seniority premium</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">With 15+ years</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td><td class="px-1.5 text-center">Yes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Example 1: resigning after 6 months</h2>
      <p>Salary of $15,000 a month ($500 a day), from 1 January to 30 June 2026 (181 days):</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Aguinaldo: 500 × 15 × 181 ÷ 365 = $3,719.18</li>
        <li>Vacation: 500 × 12 × 181 ÷ 365 = $2,975.34</li>
        <li>Vacation premium: 25% of $2,975.34 = $743.84</li>
        <li><strong>Finiquito: $7,438.36</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Example 2: unjustified dismissal after 3 years and 8 months</h2>
      <p>Salary of $12,000 a month ($400 a day), from 1 March 2023 to 31 October 2026. In the fourth year the worker is entitled to 18 vacation days, so the integrated daily salary is 400 × (365 + 15 + 18 × 0.25) ÷ 365 = $421.37.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Finiquito (aguinaldo, vacation and premium): $11,038.36</li>
        <li>3 months' pay: 90 × $421.37 = $37,923.29</li>
        <li>Seniority premium: 12 × 3.67 years × $400 = $17,635.07</li>
        <li><strong>Total: $66,596.72</strong>, or $97,558.75 if the 20 days per year also apply.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>How the total in example 2 breaks down:</p>
      <SplitBar label="Breakdown of example 2" parts={[{ label: "Finiquito", value: 11038.36, display: "$11,038.36" }, { label: "3 months’ pay", value: 37923.29, display: "$37,923.29" }, { label: "Seniority premium", value: 17635.07, display: "$17,635.07" }]} />

      <AdSlot unit="0000000000" label="Advertisement" />

      <h2>Is the finiquito taxed?</h2>
      <p>Part of it is free of ISR (Income Tax Law, art. 93): the aguinaldo up to 30 UMA ($3,519.30), the vacation premium up to 15 UMA ($1,759.65), and severance and seniority premium up to 90 UMA per year worked ($10,557.90 per year). Your employer withholds ISR on the rest, so the payment can be lower than the calculator's gross total.</p>

      <h2>If you were dismissed: what to do, step by step</h2>
      <Steps items={[
        { title: "Ask for the dismissal notice in writing", text: "Your employer must give you the reason and the date in writing (art. 47). Without it, the dismissal counts as unjustified." },
        { title: "Work out what you are owed", text: "Use the calculator above and keep the result." },
        { title: "Don’t sign anything that doesn’t add up", text: "Check the amounts before you sign a resignation or a finiquito receipt." },
        { title: "Get free advice", text: "PROFEDET advises you on your case at no cost." },
        { title: "Go to the Conciliation Center within 2 months", text: "It is the required step before a lawsuit, and the time limit runs from the dismissal (art. 518)." },
      ]} />

      <h2>Have these ready</h2>
      <Checklist items={["Your contract or offer letter", "Your latest payslips (CFDI)", "Your exact start date", "Vacation days you didn’t take", "The dismissal notice or your resignation", "An official ID"]} />

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Do I get severance if I resign?</summary><p>No. If you resign you only get the finiquito. With 15 or more years at the company, you also get the seniority premium.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>How long do I have to claim if I was dismissed?</summary><p>Two months from the dismissal (art. 518). Before filing a lawsuit you must go to your state's Conciliation Center or the Federal Conciliation Center. PROFEDET advises workers for free.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Do I have to sign a resignation to get my finiquito?</summary><p>No. Your employer must pay the finiquito even if you sign nothing. Check the amounts before you sign any document.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Which salary is used?</summary><p>The finiquito uses your daily salary. The 3 months and the 20 days per year use the integrated daily salary, which adds the daily share of your aguinaldo and vacation premium.</p></details>
    </Fragment>
  </ToolPage>
</Layout>
